---
import FolioLayout from '@dunn.dev/reference/folio-site/FolioLayout.astro';
import favicon from '../../public/favicon.svg?raw';
import Colophon from '../components/Colophon.astro';
import { nav } from '../site.ts';
import '../styles/site.css';
import '../styles/figures.css';
import Figure from '@dunn.dev/reference/folio-guide/Figure.astro';
import mark from '../brand/hero-mark.svg?raw';
---
<FolioLayout name="keystone" nav={nav} mark={favicon} description="keystone is a Tang server for clevis network-bound disk encryption, run as a Cloudflare Worker.">
  <header class="cover">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div>
        <p class="folio">Disk unlock server</p>
        <h1 class="display">keystone</h1>
        <p class="deck">keystone is a <a href="https://github.com/latchset/tang" rel="external">Tang</a> server for <a href="https://github.com/latchset/clevis" rel="external">clevis</a> network-bound disk encryption, run as a <a href="https://workers.cloudflare.com/" rel="external">Cloudflare Worker</a>. A disk unlocks at boot with no typed passphrase. Delete a stolen computer's key set and its disk stays locked. <a href="/setup/">Set it up</a>, read the <a href="/architecture/">architecture</a> or the <a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/keystone" rel="external">source</a>.</p>
        <p class="cta-row">
          <a class="cta" href="/setup/">Install</a>
          <a href="/architecture/">Architecture</a>
          <a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/keystone" rel="external">Source</a>
        </p>
        <p class="scroll-cue">What it does, below</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cover-fig" set:html={mark} />
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="sec" id="what">
    <div class="wrap chapter-grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What it does</h2>
        <p>An encrypted disk asks for its passphrase at every start. A server with no one at the keyboard waits at the prompt.</p>
        <p>keystone supplies the <a href="#secrets">missing half</a> of a second key the computer keeps on the disk, so the disk opens without the passphrase. It suits computers that reach the internet at boot but have no unlock server on their network.</p>
        <p>Until you delete a stolen computer's key set, anyone who starts it online unlocks the disk, unless the set limits <a href="#origin-gate">who can ask</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <Figure fig="15-ov1" />
        <p>At boot the computer asks keystone for the missing half of its disk key.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="how-it-works">
    <div class="wrap prose proc">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <div class="proc-body">
      <ol class="how how-fluid">
        <li>
          <p class="mark">1 · bind</p>
          <h3>Bind the disk once</h3>
          <p>clevis adds a second way into the LUKS disk, tied to a key set: two keys keystone holds under one name.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="mark">2 · boot</p>
          <h3>Unlock at startup</h3>
          <p>The initramfs, which runs before the disk opens, asks keystone for its part of the key and opens the disk.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="mark">3 · delete</p>
          <h3>Delete a stolen disk's key set</h3>
          <p>keystone then stops answering for that set, and the disk stays locked.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="sec-note">
        <p>If keystone is offline or past its daily request limit, the disk asks for its passphrase.</p>
        <Figure fig="13-failure" />
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="secrets">
    <div class="wrap prose proc">
      <h2>Where the secrets are</h2>
      <div class="proc-body cols">
      <p>keystone never receives the disk's passphrase or its second key. It keeps each key set's private keys as one secret in your Cloudflare account, and the computer sends its header value <a href="/architecture/#unlock">blinded</a>, so keystone never sees it.</p>
      <p>Anyone who can deploy keystone in your account can read the key sets. A copied key set opens its disk even after a delete, and <a href="/architecture/#rollback">rolling back</a> the Worker past a delete serves the deleted set again. Protect the account and your offline copy like the disks. To split trust across two servers, see the <a href="/architecture/#trust">architecture</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose split">
      <div id="origin-gate">
      <h2>Who can ask</h2>
      <p>A key set can name the networks allowed to unlock it and who approves the rest. Other networks are held while the approvers are mailed a link; an approval lets that network unlock for 10 minutes. Listed networks are never held. The <a href="/architecture/#gate">architecture</a> has the mechanism.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="what-you-need">
      <h2>What you need</h2>
      <ul class="key-list">
        <li>A Linux computer with a LUKS disk and clevis.</li>
        <li>A Cloudflare account and a domain on it. The free plan works with <code>--curve P-256</code>; the default curve needs the paid Workers plan.</li>
        <li>Node.js, git and jq, to create and deploy key sets.</li>
        <li>An offline place for key sets, such as pass.</li>
        <li>Each disk's passphrase, for when keystone cannot answer.</li>
      </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="next">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ul class="next">
        <li><p class="kicker">Reference</p><a href="/architecture/">Architecture</a><p>Each mechanism in figures.</p></li>
        <li><p class="kicker">Setup</p><a href="/setup/">Install</a><p>Deploy keystone and bind a first disk.</p></li>
        <li><p class="kicker">Code</p><a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/keystone" rel="external">Source</a><p>Shared as-is: no roadmap, no support promise.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Colophon slot="colophon" />
  <script>import '../scripts/figures.js';</script>
</FolioLayout>
